@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<> a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "howto/virtuoso-local-dba-passphrase-retrieval.ttl"@en ;
    schema:description "The local Virtuoso dba password is retrieved only from its distinct macOS Keychain item virtuoso-local-dba. The historical temporary hand-off file was deleted; it is not a fallback. If Keychain access is unavailable, stop and ask the user to resolve it. Never reuse or overload another secret's Keychain item."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:author <https://linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this> ;
    schema:about :howto .

:howto a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "Resolve the local Virtuoso dba password from its Keychain item only"@en ;
    schema:description "Procedure for authenticating isql/iSQL sessions against the local Virtuoso instance (port 1111, account dba) without hardcoding or re-eliciting the password each session."@en ;
    schema:step :step-keychainItem, :step-canonicalSnippet, :step-verificationFailed, :step-redact, :step-memoryGraphsViaDba, :step-memoryGraphsWebidTls ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl>,
        <../core.ttl> .

:step-keychainItem a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Keychain item virtuoso-local-dba, service/account pattern identical to uriburner-p12 but a DISTINCT item — never reuse or overload one keychain item for two different secrets"@en ;
    schema:text """CREATE (user-run, interactive, value never passes through the agent):
  security add-generic-password -U -s virtuoso-local-dba -a "$USER" -T /usr/bin/security -w

READ:
  security find-generic-password -s virtuoso-local-dba -a "$USER" -w

MIGRATED 2026-08-19: value copied from /tmp/uyi (single line, DBA_PWD=... at the time) into this new item by the agent, as a one-time historical migration into Keychain; the temporary hand-off was subsequently deleted and was not retained as a credential fallback. Verified byte-for-byte equal to the source file immediately after write (compared the two values in-shell, printed only a match/mismatch boolean, never the value itself). This mirrors :step-keychainPrimary in p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl exactly — same -T /usr/bin/security requirement to avoid a blocking GUI prompt on non-interactive reads, same -U-is-overwrite caution, same never-print-the-value rule."""@en .

:step-canonicalSnippet a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Canonical Keychain-only snippet for a local Virtuoso dba session"@en ;
    schema:text """VIRT_DBA_PWD="$(security find-generic-password -s virtuoso-local-dba -a "$USER" -w)" || {
  echo "Keychain lookup failed; stop and ask the user to resolve secure-store access." >&2
  exit 1
}
if [ -z "$VIRT_DBA_PWD" ]; then
  echo "Keychain returned no password; stop and ask the user to resolve secure-store access." >&2
  exit 1
fi
export VIRT_DBA_PWD
isql 1111 dba "$VIRT_DBA_PWD" exec="..."
unset VIRT_DBA_PWD

Always unset immediately after the isql call. Never echo, never pass on a logged command line, never write into RDF memory or the transcript. Do not use a temporary plaintext credential file if the Keychain item is unavailable."""@en .

:step-verificationFailed a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "HISTORICAL 2026-08-19: the one-time migration value was stale; the user corrected it and the Keychain-sourced credential then authenticated"@en ;
    schema:text """Initial migration (first value in /tmp/uyi, 12 bytes) returned:
  *** Error 28000: VD CL034: Bad login
The Keychain item was independently verified to hold the exact same value as /tmp/uyi at that time (string-compared before testing), so the migration itself had not corrupted anything — the /tmp/uyi value was already wrong or stale. Only ONE login attempt was made per value tested; no retry loop, because repeated failed dba logins risk triggering a Virtuoso account lockout.

User then updated /tmp/uyi (new DBA_PWD value, 19 bytes). Re-tested directly from the file first (single isql attempt) — succeeded. Re-ran the same `security add-generic-password -U ...` migration to sync the Keychain item to the corrected value, verified equal to the file, then re-tested login using the KEYCHAIN-sourced value (not the file) — also succeeded. The Keychain item was confirmed as the working source on 2026-08-19. The temporary hand-off file was deleted and must not be treated as an alternative source."""@en .

:step-redact a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Never store the dba password in RDF memory, reports, or chat"@en ;
    schema:text "Record only WHICH source resolved it and whether the isql connection succeeded. Never copy the value into preferences.ttl, session TTL, howto files, commit messages, or chat."@en .

:step-memoryGraphsViaDba a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "All agent interaction with the local memory graphs — reads AND writes — goes through the dba account, credential resolved from this Keychain item"@en ;
    schema:text """Kingsley, 2026-09-18: 'Re memory graphs, interact with them via dba account (read or write).' Stated after the SessionStart hook's anonymous SPARQL bootstrap failed (https://localhost/sparql -> HTTP 401; http://localhost:8890/sparql -> HTTP 406) and the fallback dumped 75 KB of file content that was truncated to a 2 KB preview. Do not query the memory graphs anonymously and do not propose granting anonymous/SPARQL-user read access as the fix. Resolve the password with the Step 2 snippet (security find-generic-password -s virtuoso-local-dba -a \"$USER\" -w), use it for exactly one isql session or one authenticated HTTP request, then unset it. Same guards as Steps 1-4: one login attempt per value (no retry loop — lockout risk), never echo or log the value, never put it in a URL, RDF, commit message or chat. The same rule applies to scripts that read the memory graphs (load_memory.py, full-store-graph-gate.py): they authenticate as dba via this Keychain item rather than querying anonymously."""@en .

:step-memoryGraphsWebidTls a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "6"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "WebID-TLS is an equally valid way to authenticate to the memory graphs — but only on a listener that actually requests a client certificate; verify that before trusting a 200"@en ;
    schema:text """Kingsley, 2026-09-18: 'authentication shouldn't be strictly digest, it can also be WebID-TLS.' Credentials: principal bundle core.ttl :userPrincipalCredentials (cert.p12), passphrase from Keychain uriburner-p12, handed to curl on stdin as a -K config (cert-type/cert/pass), never argv.

LOCAL FINDINGS 2026-09-18 (Virtuoso pid listening on 1111/1112/8890/443/4443):
- https://localhost/sparql-auth is Digest-only (WWW-Authenticate: Digest realm=\"SPARQL\"); a client cert alone gets 401.
- https://localhost (443) never requests a client certificate (openssl s_client: 'No client certificate CA names sent'), so a cert is never sent there; a 200 on /sparql with --cert is ANONYMOUS access, not WebID-TLS. Proof: the unscoped GRAPH ?g COUNT probe returned 401 there with the cert, identical to anonymous.
- localhost:4443 fails the handshake with the system curl (LibreSSL 3.3.6: 'tlsv1 alert protocol version'), and an openssl s_client probe hung; untested with an OpenSSL-backed curl.
CLARIFICATION (Kingsley, same day): 'Regarding WebID-TLS, this is for communications via iSQL' — and the dba credential is also for iSQL; 'iSQL is another protocol routing option'. So there are two protocol ROUTES (HTTP SPARQL and iSQL) and two IDENTITY methods (dba username/password and WebID-TLS). iSQL + dba: isql 1111 dba <pwd> (verified working 2026-09-18; CSV=ON yields parseable rows). iSQL + WebID-TLS: isql <host>:<ssl-port> "" <p12-pass> -X <cert.p12> -T <ca-bundle> -W <webid> (mtls-curl skill Mode B; the local SSL iSQL port is presumably 1112 — not yet tested, CA bundle for the local server cert not yet identified). HTTP + dba Digest on /sparql-auth is what load_memory.py and full-store-graph-gate.py use today. The HTTPS 443 findings above only show WebID-TLS is not an HTTP option on this instance.

HOOK WIRING: load_memory.py reads AGENT_RDF_MEMORY_AUTH (default 'webid-tls,digest'). webid-tls is only attempted when AGENT_RDF_MEMORY_WEBID_ENDPOINT is set explicitly — there is no default endpoint, precisely because a cert-ignoring listener would silently degrade to anonymous. A 401 disables that method for the rest of the run (lockout guard) and the next method is tried; verified 2026-09-18 that webid-tls against :443 fell through to digest."""@en .
